after two weeks at sea climate activists  Greta toon burg arrives in New York the  sixteen year old doesn't fly so she  sailed across the Atlantic to attend the  United Nations climate summit  [Music]  [Applause]  despite the sea legs making dry land a  challenge she says it's been worth it  the ground is still shaking for me the  climate and ecological crisis is a  global crisis and the biggest crisis  humanity has ever faced and if we don't  manage to work together to cooperate and  to to work together despite our  differences then we will fail so we need  to to stand together and support each  other and and to take action because  otherwise it might be too late so let's  not wait any longer let's do it now it  hasn't been a straightforward journey  since the departure from Plymouth  there's been criticism too after it  emerged that crew members from the  melissia yacht would have to fly to New  York to sail it home again when we spoke  in Stockholm earlier this year she says  the constant scrutiny is just part of  what she believes is a fight for  survival does it ever feel like too much  of a burden to be the face of a global  movement I mean of course and I I think  I speak on behalf of all the children  and young people who are school striking  and organizing that we are I mean we are  just children we shouldn't have to do  this  the adults should take that  responsibility the grete toon Berg the  UN climate summit in September a chance  for nations around the world to rise to  that challenge as the window for making  an impact continues to narrow Hannah  Thomas Peter Sky News
 

